    "q-74911": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74911",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}=$",
      "worked_solution": "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(4,-4,-2)$ does not cause the  denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(4,-4,-2)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (4,-4,-2)}\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{x-y}}{z} & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{4-(-4)}}{(-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{8}}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{-2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
