    "q-74953": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74953",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Find  ${lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{1+(-1)}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}+xz+xy+yz}{x+z} & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{x(x+z)+y(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}\\frac{(x+y)(x+z)}{x+z} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}(x+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (1,1,-1)}x+y & =1+1 \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
