    "q-74959": {
      "dom_id": "step-q74959",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{6+3}-3}{6+3-{3}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{\\sqrt{6+y}-x}{6+y-{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y}-x)(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{(\\sqrt{6+y})^{2}-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{6+y-{x}^{2}}{(6+y-{x}^{2})(\\sqrt{6+y}+x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (3,3)}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+y}+x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6+3}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\frac{1}{6}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
