    "q-129402": {
      "dom_id": "step-q129402",
      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "Determine ${lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{({0}^{2})(0)}{{0}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}(0)}{{x}^{4}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y={x}^{2}}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}({x}^{2})}{{x}^{4}+({x}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{4}}{{x}^{4}+{x}^{4}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{4}}}{2\\cancel{{x}^{4}}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{4}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$",
